  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reason focuses on comparing probabilities of obtaining a favorable outcome between the two lotteries. In Lottery A, the outcomes are 2500 Euros (33%), 2400 Euros (66%), and 0 Euros (1%). In Lottery B, the outcome is 2400 Euros with 100% probability. A favorable outcome can reasonably be interpreted as a non-zero monetary gain, which occurs with 99% probability in Lottery A (33% + 66%) and 100% in Lottery B. Alternatively, if 'favorable outcome' is interpreted as the highest possible gain (2500 Euros), then Lottery A offers a 33% chance, while Lottery B offers 0%. The decision reason would thus depend on how 'favorable outcome' is defined—either as any positive payoff or as the maximum payoff. The comparison of probability differences is feasible, so the reason is applicable.",
  "step_2": "The individual considers the probability of getting a 'high win' (\"hohen Gewinn\") and states it is 99.9% in this case (though this number does not match the actual probabilities given—possibly a misstatement). The individual chooses Lottery A based on this perceived high probability of a favorable outcome. The justification centers on the likelihood of achieving a desirable result, interpreted here as a 'high win'. However, the actual probability of the highest outcome (2500 Euros) in Lottery A is only 33%, not 99.9%. The statement suggests a possible confusion or misrepresentation of the outcomes—perhaps conflating the 99% chance of winning either 2500 or 2400 in Lottery A with a 'high win'. The individual does not mention expected value, risk aversion, or other decision rules, focusing solely on the probability of a favorable (high) outcome.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason emphasizes comparing probability differences and favors the lottery with the higher probability of a favorable outcome. If 'favorable outcome' means any positive payoff, then Lottery B has a 100% chance versus 99% in A, so the reason would prefer B. But the individual chooses A, contradicting this interpretation. However, if 'favorable outcome' means the highest possible payoff (2500 Euros), then only Lottery A offers it (33% vs 0%), and the reason would prefer A. The individual’s verbal report refers to a 'high win' and selects A, aligning with this interpretation. Despite the inaccurate 99.9% figure, the intent appears to be justifying A based on the chance of a high reward. Thus, the individual seems to focus on the probability of a favorable (i.e., high) outcome and prefers A because it offers a chance at the highest gain, consistent with the reason’s logic when 'favorable' is defined as 'high'. The information considered (probability of high win) and the justification (choosing based on high probability of favorable result) align with the decision reason, albeit with a numerical error in the report.",
  "final_assessment_score": 90
}

